---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { href } from '../../i18n/routes';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const page = 'docsindex' as const;
---
<Docs lang={lang} page={page}
  title="Documentation"
  description="Four ways in: trying the extension, setting it up for a repository, going further with enforce and AI-assisted review, and finding out why something isn't working.">
<div class="dhead stack g12">
          <p class="crumb"><b>docs</b></p>
          <h1>Documentation</h1>
          <p class="lede measure">Four ways in. Start with the one that fits what you're trying to do.</p>
        </div>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>I'm trying it out</h2>
          <p>You want to see how it works on your own pull requests, without needing anyone else's permission.</p>
          <div class="paths">
            <a class="path" href={href('install', 'en')}><span class="pn">Install the extension</span><span class="pd">Load it from a release archive, with no build tools, or build it from source. The store listings are not published yet. You'll be up and running in minutes.</span></a>
            <a class="path" href={href('labels', 'en')}><span class="pn">Labels &amp; decorations</span><span class="pd">Meet the nineteen labels, see which come from Conventional Comments and which the toolkit adds for replies, and understand when a comment becomes blocking.</span></a>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>I'm setting it up for a repository</h2>
          <p>Your team is on board. Now you want everyone following the same rules.</p>
          <div class="paths">
            <a class="path" href={href('configure', 'en')}><span class="pn">Configure</span><span class="pd">One file on the default branch. Learn which settings matter, how the three levels of configuration work together, and what the extension tells you when the file has a problem.</span></a>
            <a class="path" href={href('adoption', 'en')}><span class="pn">Adoption</span><span class="pd">assist → enforce. See what changes at each stage, what to prepare before the switch, and how to roll back in minutes.</span></a>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>I want to go further</h2>
          <p>The team writes in the convention. Now you want it to hold when someone forgets — or when part of the review is written by an agent.</p>
          <div class="paths">
            <a class="path" href={href('enforce', 'en')}><span class="pn">Enforce</span><span class="pd">What the stricter mode does — refuse to send a comment that doesn't follow the convention — and what it doesn't: it never blocks a merge, and it only applies where the extension is installed.</span></a>
            <a class="path" href={href('aireview', 'en')}><span class="pn">AI-assisted review</span><span class="pd">Session markers that say which review session wrote a comment, and the two skills that teach an agent to review, and to answer a review, in the convention.</span></a>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Something isn't working</h2>
          <p>The toolbar doesn't show up, a comment is refused, or the extension reports something you didn't expect.</p>
          <div class="paths">
            <a class="path" href={href('troubleshooting', 'en')}><span class="pn">Troubleshooting</span><span class="pd">What the status pill, the configuration notices and the diagnostic journal tell you, and the few surprises you're likely to run into the first time.</span></a>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Reference</h2>
          <p>Stable references designed for tools as well as people.</p>
          <ul class="bul">
            <li><strong>The configuration schema</strong> is available at <code>conventionalcomments.io/schema/v1.json</code>. Add it to your file's <code>$schema</code> to get autocompletion and inline validation in your editor.</li>
            <li><strong>The Conventional Comments specification</strong> lives at <a href="https://conventionalcomments.org/">conventionalcomments.org</a>. This site provides independent tooling around it and does not define or represent the specification.</li>
            <li><strong>The source code</strong> is available under Apache-2.0 and includes the normative specification and the full set of acceptance criteria.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <div class="pager"></div>
</Docs>
